ll do  this means that we don't build five  versions of a report when a filter  could solve the problem by keeping an  eye on the final solution from the  beginning  we make sure that we have the right  scope of technical investment at  every step in the process i find  for me that this comes up a lot during  the exploratory analysis phase of many  projects  because i come from a data visualization  background  i end up wasting a lot of time trying to  make my eda visualizations into  presentation worthy plots  strategic technologies also relates to  knowing  when to stop an analysis we know  that we need to avoid overfitting our  algorithms that we need to be strategic  about  when we use our heavy computing  resources we also need to be  considerate of the precision that's  required to act on the question  that we're working to answer do we need  a  directionally correct answer or is  precision required to the hundredths  place of our metric in order to make a  decision  we should aim for the minimum  specificity that's required  for making the decision and nothing more  as a data literate person you adapt your  communication styles  you base your communication on the needs  of the audience  to do that you have to know your  audience and what they're going to do  with the data product you're creating  for them  you also have to know how they're going  to engage  do they only want to know when things  are going wrong in their business  or do they want to have a regular  reminder that things are just fine  are they the type that's going to go out  and refresh a report or do they need an  email popping into their inbox with the  highlights  of course some of those specifics get  accounted for during the solution design  process but being able to anticipate  and delight our audience with something  that they need in a format that they  want  is a way to really shine as a data  expert  as a data literate person you exercise  empathy for  other perspectives while you're getting  to know your audience and what they need  you're exercising empathy you're  considering  their perspective to create something  that answers the most pressing questions  even if they haven't articulated those  questions yet yet  by stepping into their shoes you can  start to anticipate what the next  questions will be  once they see the answers to the  questions they've already asked  one of the perspectives that we should  always apply throughout an analysis is  that of the skeptic  we need to recognize that data is not  always as objective  as we want it to be there are hidden  biases and data stemming from  how it's collected analyzed and  presented  research questions can have hidden bias  as well we should approach  every new data set every new question  with a degree of skepticism  our aim is to understand why  the question is being asked and if there  are other questions that aren't being  asked that maybe should be  bias isn't inherently malicious and by  interrogating our data and our analyses  we can help ensure that we're  contributing to a more equitable  and truthful understanding of the world  so  how do we know what questions aren't  being asked  how do we learn to see through other  perspectives  it's all about breadth here we've got to  read widely we must consume a variety of  news and entertainment sources  we must practice getting into other  people's heads when i say read widely  i don't just mean non-fiction or  business books  i mean fiction if the last fiction book  you read was in school  of some kind that is your main homework  coming away from this talk is to find a  fiction book  and commit to reading it before the end  of the year  in addition to expanding our perspective  by consumption  we need to be expanding our networks to  include many different voices  this can be anyone that doesn't look or  think like you  pi data is a great way to do exactly  that so you've already made a good  choice for your own data literacy  by being here today to hear from  perspectives around the world  as a data literate person you comprehend  and critique  your work and your data you approach  problems with an  open and engaged mind coupled with a  discerning attitude  showing up in this way makes sure that  you're willing to kill your darlings  this is a familiar bit of advice given  to writers but it also applies here  it means we have to be willing to throw  away something that we've worked on  if it's getting in the way of the  overall analysis  this might mean that we exclude an  interesting but confusing visualization  of our data for the sake of clarity  it might mean that we can't do the  segmented analysis we planned because  we don't have enough data to get the  statistical power that we need  it can mean walking away from a  particular algorithm because  the tuning parameters dictate that you  would need more computing power than you  have budget for  all of these are examples of killing our  darlings in the data world  and finally as a data literate person  you translate  evidence more than just valuing evidence  you can communicate  the strength of your analysis you know  what information  is relevant to which people and you  articulate  your reasoning clearly and concisely  knowing what's relevant to people is  another part of knowing your audience  you've got to know what evidence they  value  accurately describing the strength of  our evidence is one of the most useful  things we can do when presenting an  analysis  doing this well means outlining  weaknesses clearly  knowing how to talk about statistical  power  and error terms with non-data experts is  critical  and it takes practice often business  users are not going to be interested  in the statistics lesson even if you're  exceptionally good at explaining  those things they're only going to care  about  what conclusions can we be confident  about learning how to articulate your  evidence in terms of business risk and  reward  will help you share evidence in concrete  ways without  becoming known as the statistics  professor  to recap here we have eight key  behaviors that we should be aiming for  there are a lot of things to cover here  take a look at your score sheet  for any segment where you're at least  two points away from where you think you  should be  shade that in real quick i hope you have  at least one  if not many because next we're going to  walk through some methods for building  and  exercising these behaviors i want you to  keep your octagon handy  each exercise targets multiple behaviors  and you can quickly reference your needs  to figure out which ones are going to  give you the most bang for your buck  a word of warning many of these  behaviors that we've been looking at  have  nothing to do with our technical skills  which means that some of these are going  to fall out of your comfort zone  it may even feel unproductive in the  moment that you're doing them  but in trying something new you're  already practicing one of the key  behaviors which is  constantly learning new things  let's start with flexibility and  communications  you'll want to pay particular attention  here if you have any of the bottom three  segments filled in  which are adapting new things or  learning new things  using technology strategically and  adapting communications  think about how you typically share  information  maybe you're on twitter regularly or if  you're an academic you may share your  work  in journals those two examples of  communication  are on opposite ends of the spectrum in  terms of length  but they require just as much care and  crafting  why well twitter requires conciseness  you've got to distill your thoughts into  280 characters or  chunks of 280 characters if you're gonna  do a thread  on the other end when writing something  longer  you have to craft a strong argument with  a thoughtful structure and clear logic  providing evidence that supports your  claims this requires a lot of attention  to detail  when things get really interesting is  when you start  flexing your muscles to take one type of  message  and put it into another form like taking  a tweet  and making it into a paper or taking a  paper and turning it into a tweet  and all of the different levels in  between which include emails and blog  posts and  all sorts of other articles i like to  call this communications yoga  take a small fact build a story or take  a story and  turn it into a few sentences  there are a lot of ways to do this  the first and most important part is to  intentionally get away from your usual  communications tool  do you always build powerpoint slides to  share your findings  instead try building a storyline with a  tool like  flourish or tableau you could also try  adopting the amazon model which is to  write out a briefing  memo for everyone to read in the first  few minutes of meeting  and let your words and documentation  present your argument  and what you found even if you don't end  up using this new material for a meeting  the process of getting out of your usual  methods is valuable  and you should try running meetings a  little bit differently nobody likes the  same boring structure all the time  one of my favorite ways of getting clear  on my messages is what i call the  post-it note distillation  check your octagon if you've got  adapting communication  critiquing data and work working  independently with data or collaborating  to define a problem  you should try this with your next  analysis  the objective of this activity is to  summarize the main  questions and key insights of an  analysis  onto a post-it note by restricting the  real estate  of your communication to a inch square  piece of paper  you're forced to write down only the  most important things  i like to do this with my visualizations  too if i can't draw a skeleton graph  of my key analysis outputs i'm not just  done distilling  my key information this often leads to  asking more probing questions about the  desired outcome  it's often really helpful to do this at  the beginning phases of a project  because it helps everyone get on the  same page about what is most important  both on the team and with customers  an advanced version of the post-it note  exercise is to rely  entirely on words specifically  the 1 000 most common words of your  language  this idea was inspired by a book called  thing explainer by randall the grill  if you're not familiar he draws the  webcomics  xkcd and has written a number of books  in thing explainer he uses only the 1000  most common english words to explain  how things like nuclear reactors and  rockets work  he uses simple words in greater number  to describe  things for example nuclear becomes  hard to find heavy metal but his intent  is still  clear and understandable to anyone even  if they don't know the technical  vocabulary  in the data space we might have an  analysis that required us to impute some  of our data  instead of talking about imputation  methods we might say something like  how we filled in the empty spaces with  good guesses  this clarity of idea is what we're  aiming to develop  once we can do that we can add technical  terms back in  if it is going to help us be more  concise but only if it makes sense for  our audience  on the other end of the communication  spectrum we have what i like to call  the tough q a take a peek at your  octagon  if you are looking to build empathy for  other perspectives  critique your working data and translate  evidence  this activity is for you with this  exercise we want to build  flexibility and confidence with  communicating our insights  here's how we do this first build a list  of questions that you may get about a  data product  you might already have a good list of  questions based on things you've been  asked previously  this list should include things about  the data  source the quality the frequency and  the methodology the cleaning the  analysis methods the sources of error  next put on your investigative  journalist hat and get  really skeptical elaborate your  questions  the more difficult or obscure the  questions are the better  try to twist your own work around  considering how someone  with a different background might  misinterpret your choices  once you have your list your next task  is to hunt down  and document the answers to these  questions this process is going to do  two things  one it's going to highlight the places  in your analysis  where you need to apply more rigor or  remove some bias  and two it prepares you to go into  deeper detail at a moment's notice  pairing this exercise with a  distillation exercise  helps give you a ton of flexibility in  talking about the output of your  analysis  the final skill building activity i want  to talk about is something i'm calling  practice class if you've got  independence with data  collaboration on defining problems  learning new things or exercising  empathy shaded in you'll want to host  yourself one of these  this concept stems from masterclass  where a person  shares their expertise with others who  want to learn about a concept  in this version the goal is to practice  being a beginner at something  it can often be hard to remember what it  was like to be a beginner  and it can sometimes feel intimidating  to willingly be new to something and ask  the most basic questions  so how do we do this start by forming a  small group of colleagues with a wide  array of interests  it shouldn't be particularly formal  something like a lunch gathering once a  week would be a great place to start  each person picks something that they  are an expert in an ideal topic  is something you can explain without  much preparation  is interesting to learn about and most  importantly is non-work related  examples include things like how to make  the best guacamole  or starting a bullet journal or how to  find the best travel deals  each week one person talks for 15  minutes about their selected topic  the remaining time is used for people to  ask questions of the presenter  and exercise their curiosity the goal is  to fill at least 15 more minutes with  discussion  based on questions that students ask  there are only two rules  for a practice class first the presenter  can't use  slides this rule prevents over preparing  and keeps the gatherings casual  props or a visual aid that's printed out  can be used but  try to keep everything as simple as  possible  the second rule is that students are not  allowed to use google or  any other search engine to look anything  up instead  all questions must go to the presenter  this reinforces the acceptability of  being  a novice as well as helps the entire  group be curious together  one of my favorite quotes comes from  helen mcinnis nothing is interesting  unless you're interested  by engaging with the expert in your  group on whatever they presented  you help everyone be more curious  we've covered eight key behaviors and  four skill building exercises  you've got a self-made plan on where you  need to grow your capabilities  i now have two challenges for you the  first one is this  in 2021 commit to advancing your skill  level  from where it is now to your personal  target  and at least two of these behaviors why  two  i think you're going to find it's more  challenging to move just one  so may as well go big  a second challenge you may have already  seen this one coming  because i alluded to it earlier read a  new book before the end of 2020.  i have a few recommendations for you if  you need some inspiration  some of these are fiction some are  non-fiction don't worry about  reading all these small covers you can  check out that link where i've got  two lists on goodreads that you can use  to find  a good recommendation for what you want  to read at the end of this year  finally i want this to mark the  beginning of a conversation  i want to connect with you i'd love to  hear how you're building your insight  translation skills and your data  competency skills too  i am after all a data expert like you i  love hearing about cool projects and how  data is making the world better  you can find me on social media at the  links listed here or  drop me an email let me know what you  want to hear more about and how i can  help you  thank you
 

